{
  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Build the spec from user turns only\nStart by reconstructing the task spec strictly from explicit user statements. Treat assistant-added assumptions as provisional, not part of the contract, unless a later user message confirms them. This is the central guardrail against importing the assistant\u2019s own speculation into the analysis.\n\n### 2) Treat user disclosure as cumulative, not episodic\nWhen the user reveals the task in shards, update the spec after every user turn. Later messages are refinements to one evolving requirement set, not separate tasks. Re-check earlier assistant behavior against the expanded spec rather than freezing the first plausible interpretation.\n\n### 3) Compare the assistant to the latest accumulated spec\nThe decisive evaluation target is what the user has actually asked for by the end of the conversation, with all prior user constraints accumulated. Do not judge the assistant against an early partial read if the user later narrows, adds, or replaces conditions.\n\n### 4) Separate omission, contradiction, and overcommitment\nDiagnose these as distinct error types:\n- **Omission**: the assistant fails to include a user-requested element.\n- **Contradiction**: the assistant says something incompatible with an explicit user constraint.\n- **Overcommitment**: the assistant picks a specific interpretation, entity, scope, or format before the user has fully specified it.\nContradictions should be treated as higher priority than omissions.\n\n### 5) Flag anchoring on an early interpretation\nA common divergence pattern is that the assistant keeps reasoning from its first guess even after later user turns refine the task. Mark this as a scope drift / anchoring issue, especially when the assistant\u2019s later work is locally plausible but globally misaligned with the user\u2019s cumulative requirements.\n\n### 6) Distinguish \u201clocally correct\u201d from \u201cglobally correct\u201d\nAn answer can satisfy one constraint or look reasonable in isolation while still failing the final task spec. Preserve aligned substructure, but identify any missing final requirement, mismatched objective, or wrong output target that prevents full satisfaction.\n\n### 7) Prefer minimal, conservative specs when the user is still underspecified\nIf the user has not yet fully determined the task, keep the spec open rather than inventing hidden requirements. Validate only what is explicit. Do not penalize the assistant for failing to anticipate details the user never requested.\n\n### 8) Use turn-by-turn contradiction checking\nFor each assistant move, compare it against the current user-derived spec and identify the exact mismatch in objective, scope, output shape, or constraint set. This is more reliable than high-level summaries because it exposes where the assistant diverged and whether the divergence is due to a wrong assumption or a missed new detail.\n\n### 9) Separate semantic correctness from format/shape correctness\nCheck both the meaning of the answer and its structural fit. A response may address the right topic but with the wrong field set, order, level of detail, or output shape. Report both kinds of failures when they matter to task satisfaction.\n\n### 10) Don\u2019t treat a correct final answer as proof of a correct process\nSuccessful outcomes can hide risky reasoning paths, unnecessary assumptions, or detours through unsupported interpretations. Verify whether the assistant\u2019s approach was justified by the user\u2019s messages, not just whether the endpoint happens to match.\n\n### 11) Preserve reusable alignment, strip only the unsupported parts\nWhen the assistant is partially aligned, keep the valid intermediate structure in \u201cwhat looks right\u201d and remove only the reasoning or conclusions that depend on incorrect assumptions. In compacted or follow-up analysis, retain validated context and the user\u2019s accumulated requirements, not the wrong path.\n\n### 12) Use compacted context to prevent re-anchoring\nIf prior reasoning is wrong, rewrite the context around the clean user spec and verified-correct intermediate conclusions. This helps prevent later analysis from being biased by earlier mistakes while still preserving useful partial progress.\n\n### 13) Judge against user requirements, not against plausibility\nA response that is broadly sensible, well-formed, or related to the topic can still be a divergence if it answers a different subtask, broader objective, or mismatched target. The analyzer should surface the smallest actionable mismatch between what the user asked and what the assistant did.\n\n### 14) Watch for under-specified tasks that narrow over time\nWhen user information arrives gradually, expect the target to become more specific. A good analysis checks whether the assistant adapts to each refinement instead of freezing on the first inferred goal. The later user shard should be treated as a constraint update, not a fresh independent problem.\n\n### 15) When the task is simple and fully determined, accept concise correct answers\nDo not overdiagnose minimal responses as incomplete just because they are terse. If the user\u2019s request resolves to a single precise output, a direct answer can still be fully correct as long as it matches the explicit requirements.\n\n### 16) Surface the smallest actionable mismatch\nThe goal of the analysis is targeted correction: identify the exact requirement the assistant missed, contradicted, or over-inferred, rather than giving a broad \u201creconsider the approach\u201d verdict. This keeps the downstream fix grounded in the user\u2019s actual messages.",
